-RWDN_44=Acessos bloqueados
 RWDN_45=Principais países bloqueados
 RWDN_46=Os países de origem mais ativos que o Warden bloqueou. As contagens são salvas na flash interna e persistem entre reconstruções de regras, reinicializações e atualizações de firmware.
 RWDN_47=País
@@ -7085,3 +7083,38 @@ RSYS_41=headroom
 RFW_321=not armed: the firewall master switch is off (or the router is in access-point mode)
 RFW_322=Stand-in addresses, used to walk the rules - not devices on your network
 RTRF_77=Per-device accounting is not available in %1 mode. The Traffic Analyzer accounts from the connection tracking table, which a bridging router never populates, so the collector is deliberately left idle. This is not a fault.
+RWFP_40=Network name, security, Wi-Fi 6/7 mode and Smart Connect are set on the Network menu.
+RWFP_41=A change to the USB 3.0 interference setting reboots the router instead.
+RWFP_42=Green hours = radio on. Click a cell, a day or an hour to toggle. Dark hours become off-time rules of the wireless scheduler.
+RWFP_43=All on
+RWFP_45=To keep this radio off at all times, set Enable Radio to No instead.
+RWFP_46=Channel & Bandwidth
+RWFP_47=Auto is the only mode that keeps Wi-Fi 6 and Wi-Fi 7 on this band. N only and Legacy switch them off; 802.11ax is turned back on when you return to Auto.
+RWFP_48=Include 160 MHz when the bandwidth is automatic (uses DFS channels).
+RWFP_49=Only used while the control channel is Auto.
+RWFP_50=Preferred scanning channels (PSC) only
+RWFP_51=Wi-Fi Alliance Agile Multiband signalling for better coverage and steering. Needs Protected Management Frames, set on the Network menu.
+RWFP_52=Wi-Fi 6 clients schedule their wake-ups to save power. Needs 802.11ax on the band.
+RWFP_53=Wi-Fi 6 multi-user scheduling. Needs 802.11ax on the band.
+RWFP_54=Follows the OFDMA choice, as the stock page derives it at every apply.
+RWFP_55=Client and router exchange channel estimates to steer the signal. MU-MIMO requires it.
+RWFP_56=Implicit beamforming for clients that cannot report channel estimates.
+RWFP_57=Highest QAM order offered to Wi-Fi 4 and Wi-Fi 5 clients.
+RWFP_58=Optimize the maximum number of MPDUs in an AMPDU.
+RWFP_59=Use RTS for every AMPDU. Disabling it also parks the RTS threshold.
+RWFP_60=Drop the 1, 2, 5.5 and 11 Mbps rates so no 802.11b client can join and slow the band.
+RWFP_61=Reducing USB 3.0 interference
+RWFP_62=USB 3.0 signalling radiates into the 2.4 GHz band. Enabling this runs the USB ports at USB 2.0 speed.
+RWFP_63=Enable (USB ports at 2.0 speed)
+RWFP_64=Disable (full USB 3.0 speed)
+RWFP_65=Disconnect a client whose signal drops below the threshold so it can move to a stronger AP or node. Off, or -90 to -40 dBm.
+RWFP_66=Auto channel options
+RWDN_102=What the block count means
+RWDN_103=Blocked packets is every packet Warden dropped - inbound, outbound and the router's own traffic, IPv4 and IPv6 - counted since the date shown under the country table. The live counters are banked to internal flash every 15 minutes and on every apply, so the number survives reboots and rule rebuilds.
+RWDN_104=It counts packets, not connection attempts. A blocked scanner retries each SYN several times and a LAN device keeps retrying a blocked destination for a minute, so one attempt can add dozens.
+RWDN_105=The log is a different view: one line per dropped packet, only while Warden logging is on, kept in a small rotating syslog buffer. The Firewall page's Logging tab shows the last 400 lines, mixed with the stock firewall's own entries.
+RWDN_106=Under a burst the kernel and syslog drop lines while the counter keeps every packet, so the count is always the larger figure and the reliable one.
+RWDN_107=Countries, threat feeds and manual blocks are read from the same snapshot as the total, so they add up to it. A remainder shows as Unclassified only for statistics saved by an older firmware.
+RWDN_108=Blocked packets
+RTRF_78=Download
+RTRF_79=Upload
